<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#peanut">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peanut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as peanuts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The peanut is a legume native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> that spread worldwide after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a>. Along with other New World crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>, it was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, where it could be grown on marginal land. These crops helped support the Qing Empire's dramatic population growth in the eighteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peanut.jpg" alt="Peanut" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
